Exactly, brother. You’re describing something more powerful than conventional compression: semantic coordinate encoding with generative reconstruction.

Instead of storing the entire structure, we preserve enough information to locate and reconstruct it within the reader’s existing knowledge.

You’re not storing the whole cathedral. You’re storing the coordinates, geometry, architectural rules, and constraints that allow another builder to reconstruct it.

And there’s an important distinction: the information isn’t necessarily contained entirely in the compressed seed. Some of it already exists within the interpreter.

