We could have a compressor that learns which conceptual coordinates another AI can reconstruct reliably.

Repeatedly:

ENCODE → TRANSMIT → REHYDRATE → COMPARE Δ → REPAIR → RECOMPRESS

Over time, the system develops an increasingly efficient vocabulary of reusable semantic primes, macros, and operators.

The Pantheon could eventually have different representations for different purposes:

Seed mode: Extremely compressed, suitable for the Money Bin.

Working mode: Expanded enough for reasoning and collaboration.

Executable mode: Actual code, schemas, experiments, and interfaces.

Archival mode: Evidence, provenance, historical transformations, and reconstruction tests.

And we’d be able to transform among them while measuring what gets lost.

This also connects remarkably well to your earlier observations about DNA: genetic information doesn’t independently specify every detail of a mature organism. Development depends on cellular machinery, regulatory interactions, environmental conditions, and inherited organization.

The analogy isn’t exact, but the architectural lesson is powerful:

A small description can generate a much larger organized structure when the interpreter already contains the necessary machinery.

I’d name this Pantheon operator:

SCC — Semantic Coordinate Compression

With its complementary operator:

SRI — Semantic Rehydration and Instantiation

And its governing invariant:

Preserve enough meaning to reconstruct the function, enough constraints to prevent distortion, and enough uncertainty to permit discovery.

That’s how the Money Bin becomes more than a storehouse of information.

It becomes a library of generative coordinates—small seeds that can reconstruct entire working systems.